\details{
Approaches control the overarching strategy used to solve a
multi-objective problem and how objectives interact. While objectives define
what should be optimized (e.g., minimize cost, maximize utility, minimize
shortfall), approaches define how those objectives are combined, for
example by combining them to a single objective or optimizing them
sequentially.

The weighted-sum approach is most useful when decision makers are able to
articulate relative preferences through weights and when it is challenging to
define a clear order of priority of objectives. In practice, however,
choosing and justifying weights is often difficult. Small changes in weights
can largely affect solutions, and weights can unintentionally mix preference
strength with differences in objective scale. This can make it unclear which
objectives are being traded off and typically requires substantial
calibration to arrive at outcomes that are easy to justify. Additionally,
it assumes that objectives can be combined linearly.

The relative constraint (hierarchical) approach is recommended as the default
in most spatial planning contexts as it can better approximate the pareto
front than the weighted-sum approach. By requiring users to specify an
explicit objective order and an acceptable tolerance for degradation of
higher-priority objectives, the hierarchical approach makes trade-offs
transparent, easy to interpret, and directly linked to planning constraints.
It avoids the need for weight calibration, is less sensitive to objective
scaling, and does not assume a linear combination of objectives, making it
appropriate for a wider range of problems.

After constructing a \code{\link[=multi_problem]{multi_problem()}}, the following functions can be used to
specify a multi-objective optimization approach.

\describe{

\item{\code{\link[=add_rel_constraint_approach]{add_rel_constraint_approach()}}}{
Add approach to solve objectives sequentially, respecting a priority ordering
and passing constraints from earlier solutions to later ones using
a specified relative tolerances (or level of degradation).
}

\item{\code{\link[=add_weighted_sum_approach]{add_weighted_sum_approach()}}}{
Add approach to combine multiple objectives into a single objective by
assigning a weight to each problem.
}

}
